Abstract:
Many applications in engineering, science and society require us to make decision with uncertain outcomes. A fundamental problem is the exploration-exploitation dilemma: The need to trade off  gathering information (explore) and making decisions that have proven to be effective in the past (exploit). In many real world tasks, the number of choices is extremely large, and thus we need to make inferences about the outcome of decisions given past experiences. 

Due to its central importance, the exploration-exploitation dilemma has been tackled by several communities: The theory community developed principled algorithms with strong guarantees for the multi-armed bandit problem (a classical paradigm formalizing the exploration-exploitation tradeoff) as well as generalizations; application-oriented researchers have developed practical and effective algorithms for response surface methods and Bayesian global optimization. However, there is a gap between the wide applicability of the practical methods, and the theoretical rigor of the multi-armed bandit approaches.

This proposal lays out a basic research program, with the goal of bridging the gap between theory and practice by developing novel approaches towards solving the exploration-exploitation dilemma in complex, structured domains that cannot be solved using any of the existing approaches. We build on recent techniques from nonparametric and high-dimensional statistics, as well as analytical techniques from online optimization, in order to develop new, principled algorithms for solving decision making problems in real applications. 

Our research will pursue the following inter-related threads:
(1) New algorithms for dealing with uncertain, high-dimensional domains by exploiting recent results from high-dimensional statistics;
(2) New algorithms that learn to optimize from task context and past experiences;
(3) The investigation of fundamental questions on the parallelization of Bayesian global optimization;
(4) The evaluation of the algorithms on real-world applications including information gathering on the web, environmental monitoring and neuroprosthetics.

The expected impact of the proposed project includes the development of new theoretical insights and tools, their realization in novel algorithms, their implementation as open source software, and their application in challenging real-world exploration-exploitation tasks. The success of the project is backed by a significant amount of preliminary research results by the PI and his group.  Beyond the theoretical and algorithmic insights, the application of the techniques pursued in this proposal can potentially have immediate benefits for society.

Quantitative forecasts of large-scale political violence – especially civil wars – are becoming increasingly important, both in academia and the policy community. However, while recent research has made significant progress in forecasting the outbreak of conflict, the problem of predicting conflict trajectories once fighting has begun has received little attention. This proposal argues that this gap is caused primarily by a lack of suitable data. A significant body of literature shows that the duration, intensity, and geographical scope of civil conflicts is determined in large parts by the goals and capacities of the rebel groups involved, thus suggesting rebel-characteristics as a powerful predictor for future conflict dynamics. However, because existing data sets containing rebel-specific information are updated only infrequently and offer limited coverage, they are of very limited use for forecasting applications. The project described herein addresses this issue by proposing a machine coding system that collects and classifies information on rebel organizations from online sources in a largely automated manner. By building on recent advances in the fields of machine learning and natural language processing, the proposed system leverages the unprecedented amount of openly available content on the internet to collect information on non-state conflict participants in near real-time. The machine coding framework introduced in this proposal consists of three modules:
(1) A named entity resolution system that detects newly emerging political organizations in online news stories;
(2) A text classification approach for learning rebel groups’ political goals, ideological commitments, and constituencies from encyclopedic narratives;
(3) An information extraction system that collects data on rebel groups’ military successes and losses from online news reports.

Glacial habitats host an astonishing diversity of species and life forms; however, most of the world’s mountain glaciers are melting due to climate change, threatening glacier biodiversity and the functioning of mountain ecosystems. In Europe, glacier retreat is particularly severe for the southernmost, peripheral chains, where the smallest glaciers occur. The European Habitat Directive includes Permanent Glaciers in the list of habitats deserving conservation, and glacial habitats host several endemic species. However, none of these species is listed in the Habitat Directive, and information on biodiversity of these environments is scarce, hindering our ability to manage mountain socio-ecological systems.
PrioritIce aims at identifying trends, threats and processes acting on the biodiversity associated to glacial habitats (i.e. species living above the glacier or in glacier forelands) in Europe, with a focus on Alps and peripheral chains hosting critically endangered habitats. By combining traditional and molecular (environmental DNA) approaches, our consortium has already gathered data on 1) the distribution of key taxa (bacteria, fungi, nematodes, tardigrades, earthworms, arthropods, plants), and 2) a range of ecosystem functions (pollination, soil respiration and nutrients) from 52 study sites across the European Alps and southern European mountains. Building on these data, we will:
1) provide an exhaustive assessment of the taxonomic and functional diversity of organisms living in glacial habitats. We will complement the rich database of the consortium with additional samples from peripheral glaciers, with a special focus on poorly known endemic species;
2) analyse biological interactions to identify how species contribute to ecosystem functioning and services in glacial habitats. We will build on existing databases and develop machine learning protocols focusing on the impact of glacier extinction on the diversity–functioning relationship;
3) provide evidence-based priority programs and actions for managing glacial habitats and devising strategies for anticipating the consequences of glacier retreat under climate change scenarios. Together with a broad set of stakeholders, we will inform decision-making and environmental policy to preserve, support, and manage these ecosystems.
Studying the distribution, abundance and functions of biodiversity across mountain environments, PrioritIce will enhance evidence-based protection approaches and shed light on the conservation of a poorly known, unprotected biota. Integrating statistical modelling approaches with innovative molecular methodologies will allow us to improve the ecological representation of biodiversity and its role in the functioning of glacial habitats in order to identify priority conservation areas. This will provide practitioners, managers and policy makers with the much-needed knowledge for defining adequate strategies for preserving biodiversity and mountain ecosystems.

A central challenge in decision making in science and engineering is the explore–exploit dilemma: The need to trade exploration (i.e., collecting information) and exploitation (i.e., making decisions identified as effective in the past). Oftentimes, the number of alternatives is vast, and consequently we must reason about and predict the outcome of untried decisions based on the collected information.

Since it plays such a central role, the explore–exploit dilemma has been addressed by several communities: Theoreticians have developed principled algorithms with performance guarantees for what is known as the multi-armed bandit problem (a classical formalization of the exploration–exploitation tradeoff) and its generalizations; practitioners have designed efficient algorithms for Bayesian optimization and the design of computer experiments that work well on certain real-world applications. For a long time, there was a large gap between the empirical success of the practical methods, and the rigor of the multi-armed bandit techniques.

This proposal is a follow-up to an SNF project: Gaussian Process Bandit Optimization: Theory and Applications, which started a research program aimed at “bridging the gap”: The goal was to design practical approaches towards solving the exploration–exploitation dilemma, that still enjoy strong theoretical guarantees. The methodology revolves around Gaussian processes, flexible, nonparametric statistical models, and their use to address challenging exploration–exploitation tradeoffs in a principled manner. This line of inquiry has proven to be very successful, with results published at premier conferences and journals, and considerable impact on real world applications in recommender systems, environmental monitoring and experimental design.

This follow-up project aims to exploit this momentum and the algorithmic insights developed previously, yet explore challenging new problems that no existing approaches can handle. In particular, we seek novel algorithms capable of accommodating complex constraints present in practical problems: Safety, Dependence and Observability. Concretely, our research will weave together the following inter-related threads:

1. Develop novel algorithms for localizing near-optimal solutions while satisfying safety constraints;
2. Push Gaussian process optimization towards more general tasks by accommodating dependency constraints, for example present in multi-stage decision and control problems;
3. Design algorithms able to operate under observability constraints arising in practical applications;
4. Evaluate the algorithms on real-world domains ranging from recommender systems to robot learning to neuroprosthetics.

In terms of expected impact, we will develop new theoretical techniques and analyses, realize them in novel algorithms, implement them as open source software, and apply them to challenging real-world applications. Success of the project is supported by substantial theoretical and empirical results by the PI and his group achieved in the prior SNF project. Beyond furthering our theoretical and algorithmic understanding of the ubiquitous explore–exploit dilemma, the application of the techniques pursued in this proposal have the potential of direct benefits for society.

The summer of 2022 will be marked in history as the moment when it became possible to create convincing digital images of our written ideas and thoughts in just a few seconds. Multimodal AI text-to-image models have recently become extraordinarily successful in creating realistic and convincing images from textual descriptions and are currently gaining enormous attention not only within the AI community, but also among a broad scope of scholars, artists and practitioners fascinated with this technology. From generating illustrations for children’s books to upholding fake news with convincing images of events that never happened - the range of potential applications is wide and manifold. But so are the various cultural, societal, ethical and political impacts of these technologies. These models are expected to have a strong impact on creative and media industries. Therefore it becomes urgently necessary to understand how and why certain sentences are transformed to specific images and what are the potential implications of the data relations encoded within those models. Why does a rainbow appear on images generated from “dark side of the moon” and why “a cashier” is almost always depicted as female? Trained on millions of image-text pairs sampled from the internet, large vision-language AI models integrate and propagate various biases, often including dominant societal perspectives and selective cultural memories. These models encode in a numerical hyperdimensional feature space (i.e. latent space) numerous and complex associations which exist between data items collected at a certain point in time. However, which associations are “remembered” and which are “forgotten” - in other words, what constitutes the canon of the latent spaces of large vision-language models and how to systematically explore them, emerges as a new and exciting research challenge. 
The aim of the proposed project is to conduct a holistic and multiperspectival analysis of multimodal AI technologies in the context of art and culture, focusing on three interweaving research directions, defined through the corresponding objectives:
O1) Explainable Latent Spaces - Develop methods to investigate socio-cultural patterns encoded in large multimodal AI models and datasets;
O2) Collaborative Practices - Define and conduct collaborative experimental practices to study the impact of multimodal AI models on artistic and cultural production and appreciation;
O3) Interdisciplinary Contextualization - Construct a theoretical framework for the integration and contextualization of multimodal AI concepts within arts and humanities.
The proposed project aims to integrate both quantitative and qualitative methods in order to tackle the specific aims of explainability, bias mitigation and cultural inclusivity in large-vision language models. Additionally, the project specifically aims to foster the understanding of multimodal AI technologies to non-AI experts and researchers in the arts and humanities. The expected results include computational methods that systematically analyze entanglements in the models’ latent spaces; novel empirical and theoretical insights into the role of large AI models for the study of art and culture; as well as advancement of methodological practices in interdisciplinary research environments. The project’s results and methodological structure is expected to be highly impactful for both the (digital) art and humanities and the AI research communities. The project aims to integrate diverse outlooks and incite cross-disciplinary debates on the globally relevant topic of AI-based content generation and its far-reaching implications on the future of art, culture and society.

TrACE (Training Assessment Competencies in English as a Second Language) is a follow-up to two previous research projects in the area of English learning: ASSET (Assessing Student’s English Texts)  and MEWS (Measuring English Writing at Secondary Level) . While ASSET was concerned with teachers’ diagnostic competences when judging English student texts, MEWS generated a large corpus of authentic learner texts with holistic ratings by trained humans and assessment software. TrACE combines these projects to achieve following objectives: 
1) Generating detailed ratings of text qualities for a large corpus of authentic student texts, achieving highest possible objectivity by combining human ratings and machine scoring (Studies 1 & 2);
2)Investigating what factors at teacher, student, text, and judgment level determine the ability of pre-service and in-service English teachers to assess complex English learner texts accurately by comparing their assessment to human- and machine-made benchmark scores (Study 3);
3) Building a comprehensive, free online training tool and evaluating its effectiveness with a large sample of pre-service English teachers (Study 4, 5 & 6).
Empirical studies have shown scoring consistency to be relatively low when raters do not use analytic assessment practices (i.e., apply detailed criteria to student texts) (Dempsey et al., 2009). Therefore, in Study 1, human raters will assess a large portion of texts from the MEWS corpus using analytic criteria developed in ASSET, relating to aspects of textual quality typically assessed by teachers in schools (Keller, 2013). These ratings serve as the basis for Study 2, in which automatic scoring models for the analytic human scores are developed using natural language processing (NLP) techniques (Rupp et al., 2019). Combinations of human and machine ratings will then be used as benchmarks in several empirical studies that aim to train teachers’ diagnostic competences.
Working with a large corpus of authentic texts will allow us to investigate key determinants of judgment accuracy as continuous variables within one single study. Study 3 will investigate the influence of teacher, student, text, and judgments characteristics on teacher judgement accuracy (Südkamp et al., 2012). The same corpus is then used to investigate the effectiveness of specific training measures: Study 4 will examine whether teachers who receive feedback on their text judgments (comparison with benchmarks) become more accurate in their assessment. Studies 5 & 6 will provide teachers with automatically generated visualized feedback on key features of text quality to determine whether this helps them to assess texts more accurately (vocabulary in Study 5 and argumentative structure in Study 6). Based on the results of these training measures, we will develop a comprehensive, free on-line training tool for pre-service and in-service teachers to develop their diagnostic competence while using detailed analytic criteria. TrACE will thus explore the concordant variables of diagnostic competence in the domain of text assessment and make a substantial contribution to the field of teacher professionalization in a national and international context.

The attributes of seismic waves, such as velocity and polarization, or the wave-field composition reflect the properties of the propagation media. Analysis of the seismic wave-field therefore enables us to gather knowledge of geological and geophysical features of the Earth’s structure. Depending on the wave type (surface waves or body waves) and frequency range, different geophysical methods can be used to resolve different levels of details in the structure. Passive seismic surveying methods represent a cost-effective alternative to expensive invasive techniques (e.g. borehole techniques) of near surface exploration. Such methods therefore represent a valuable tool in geophysical and geotechnical investigations, especially related to earthquake risk reduction. During the past decade, passive methods based on the analysis of ambient vibration wave-fields have become popular due to their success and wide range applicability in a variety of situations. Single station methods are widely used to derive the fundamental frequency of resonance f0, whereas seismic arrays are used to derive shear-wave velocity profiles of the soil columns respectively. The basis of passive surface-wave array analysis is that the frequency dependence of the surface-wave phase velocity, or the ‘dispersion relation’, can be estimated using different signal processing techniques. A model of seismic wave velocities, compatible with the observed dispersion relation and f0, is then found by solving a non-linear geophysical inverse problem. Although passive methods have been continuously developed over the last decade, there is still considerable potential for improvement due to the complexity of the problem; as outlined in our proposal.
Hazards estimates are derived from ground motion prediction equations (GMPEs) that often rely just on geological information to classify a station in terms of Vs30 or soil classes. This fact is one of the main reasons for large epistemic uncertainties in such relations and derived hazard products. Thus, there is an urgent need for cost effective procedures to derive local subsurface structure of the existing thousands of seismic stations worldwide. The methods developed within the framework of this project should help resolve these issues, improving the stations’ site classification, and therefore reducing uncertainties in hazard estimates. In particular, in this project we are interested in the development of signal processing methods for the analysis of ambient vibrations and earthquake ground motion from single stations and array recordings. Our aim is to significantly extend the analysis capabilities of the methods presently in use. The project’s primary focus lies in the methodological development of processing techniques and the application to specific products for seismic hazard assessment. Probabilistic graphical models, proposed in our project, are becoming more and more popular tools in various fields including machine learning and signal processing. In our approach we rely on factor graphs, one flavor among many graphical modeling techniques. The new wave-field decomposition method, developed recently, has several advantages when compared to other methods, and the potential for further developments is considerable. We envision improvements of this analysis method in different ways: we aim at the retrieval of additional geophysical properties of interest; to improve the accuracy of the methods; and to extend the domain of application of the passive methods to new types of measurements and depth ranges. We use both ambient vibrations and earthquake recordings. The specific goals of the project include:
1. Exploring tools to extract properties of the local structure from single station recordings;
2. Improving the accuracy of estimated wave-field parameters with arrays. This will be achieved by considering the broadband nature of seismic waves and by exploiting the time-invariability of seismic parameters over long recordings;
3. The estimation of additional quantities of interest with array recordings, such as surface wave attenuation (Q factor), directional properties of surface waves, and the composition of the wave-field;
4. Extending the domain of applications to larger arrays including temporary large arrays and data from the Swiss Seismic Networks; 
5. Application of the new methods to derive structural information for seismic stations in regional networks;

The effective treatment of metastatic melanoma is an unmet clinical need since less than 50% of patients survive at 5 years after diagnosis of advanced disease. This dismal prognosis is mainly due to the formation of brain metastases for which no effective therapies are available. Of all cancer types, melanoma has the highest rate of cerebral tropism, with over 40% of advanced patients developing brain metastases.

Clinical observations have shown that patients above 70 years old have metastatic melanomas with the most aggressive prognostic features, which result in the highest mortality rate. Surprisingly, the impact of physiological aging on the pattern of melanoma metastases is largely unexplored when studying the biological mechanisms driving cancer cell dissemination. Pre-clinical and clinical studies have shown that blood vessels and lymphatics experience structural and functional changes during physiological aging, including compromised cell-cell junctions and increased vascular permeability, particularly in the blood brain barrier (BBB). This compromised vascular function coupled with the loss of vascular-identity genes increases the transmigration of circulating melanoma cells in the brain (i.e. extravasation). However, a detailed mechanistic explanation of the higher rate of melanoma cell transmigration through the vasculature of old patients is still lacking, partially due to the absence of reliable models that:
1) recapitulate features of vascular aging;
2) allow high-throughput (HT) parametric studies to identify drivers of melanoma cell extravasation.

To fill this gap, we propose to:
1) design a high-throughput system hosting 3D perfusable human BBB minitissues;
2) compare BBB models derived from human donors of different ages and validate them at the molecular and functional level by comparison with human brain tissue biopsies and published datasets;
3) analyze the biological mechanisms driving the age-dependent extravasation of melanoma cells and provide a list of candidate targets involved in melanoma extravasation for functional perturbation;
4) perform a CRISPR-based screening of the most promising targets and validate them

A key aspect of the proposal is the multidisciplinary integration of microvascular network (MVN) biofabrication, multi-omic characterization of human brain vasculature, and design of a data analysis methodology that exploits artificial intelligence to combine experimental data with existing databases to identify and rank pathways underlying melanoma extravasation. During the project we then expect to:
1) design and microfabricate a HT platform hosting human 3D minitissues that allows to perform large-scale experimental campaigns; this platform is fully integrated with HT biofabrication strategies and HT analytical techniques, and can find applications in any other field requiring screening of compounds or drugs (e.g. combination of chemotherapies on patient-derived cells);
2) provide a detailed molecular and functional analysis of the differences characterizing the human BBB during aging, which can have consequences in other disease models including neurodegeneration;
3) identify drivers of melanoma extravasation through the BBB by combining the molecular and functional profile of vasculature and cancer cells through HT live confocal imaging, automated quantification of local vascular permeability and cutting-edge spatial transcriptomics; the design of a novel data analysis pipeline is expected to discover potential mechanisms of extravasation also shared by immune cells during inflammatory processes or other phenomena involving immune cell mobilization;
4) validate the identified targets through a functional CRISPR screening and human tissue microarrays, which provide a higher clinical relevance and are often neglected in basic biology studies.

Monitoring epileptogenic cortical areas in-vivo has been carried out in clinical environment within the context of epilepsy treatment consisting in detecting and subsequently resecting incriminated areas. The determination of the epileptogenic areas as well as the development of future implantable systems aiming at early detection or prediction of the seizure both are in need of autonomous implantable recording systems that enable patients to be monitored in their home environment. To this extent, portable and autonomous solutions to cortical implantable recording systems must be provided. No such full system exists to date, though several research groups have proposed block-level solutions, efficiently implementing some parts of the system. To date, the efficient operation and implementation of several blocks pertaining to cortical implants have been achieved resulting in a fair understanding of block-level engineering issues and their potential solution. A true system-level approach is needed to enable the systematic study and further development of cortical implants. This project is proposed as a continuation of the SNSF-funded projects No. 130166 and 14972 “Implantable Bio-Electronics for Wireless and High-Resolution Monitoring of Epilepsy in-vivo,” formally extending over the period of December 2010 through November 2014, and intends to exploit the achieved knowledge and scientific results to extend the research towards system-level issues, their modeling and the optimization of the developed system, considering a special emphasis to power management. The founding hypothesis of the proposed research consists of the necessity to provide the implant time-based partial autonomy with respect to its external base station. For example, the patients must be allowed to disconnect themselves from the external base station for several hours while trusting the implant to continue operation in automatically adapted state. This fundamental constraint on the operation of the dual consisting of the external base station and the implanted system has tremendous impact on the way the system must be conceived and controlled. The research proposes to tackle the involved issues into the development of a systematic approach to the conception and control of semi-autonomous cortical implants, and proposes to achieve the following scientific goals:
•study of a system level (top-down) development methodology based on a rigorous modeling at high and middle levels of abstraction and an optimization procedure with specific emphasize on power dissipation; the system is assumed powered by an external base station mounted on a lightweight helmet; the external base station can be disconnected from the implant which is semi-autonomous;
•method for partitioning the algorithms derived in the methodology into external, implanted hardware and software; development of an external and implanted processor supporting the control and dynamic power optimization of the implant; development of power management circuits;
•development of cortical recording patches based on a 64-channel integrated circuit embedded into a patch supporting a dense arrangement of microelectrodes; study of flexible patch technology based on wire-bonding technique extensions;
•study and development of a wireless power and data transmission system, and an implantable battery management system that fully supports the power management methodology and hardware;
•study of seizure detection and prediction algorithms, and partitioning methodology into the analog front-end sensor, and the implanted and external digital hardware based feature extraction; development of the internal memory supporting data storage in autonomous mode, and that supports the power management methodology;
•animal experiments studying the effectiveness of high-density readout electrodes, the developed patches, and study of epileptogenic signal capture and seizure detection using the proposed system.
The project aims at gathering the theoretical and development parts into one comprehensive system and methodology, that is applied to epilepsy study in-vivo and that will conduct to implantable system with the capacity of modulating or suppressing seizures. This research project is presented as part of long-term goals of the research groups, which have been active and have collaborated in the domain for several years.

Monitoring epileptogenic cortical areas in-vivo has been carried out in clinical environment within the context of epilepsy treatment consisting in detecting and subsequently resecting incriminated areas. The determination of the epileptogenic areas as well as the development of implantable systems aiming at early detection or prediction of the seizure both are in need of autonomous implantable recording systems that enable patients to be monitored in their home environment. To this extent, portable and autonomous solutions to cortical implantable recording systems must be provided. This area of research has been intensively tackled over the past few years, and research groups have proposed block-level solutions, efficiently implementing some parts of the system. To date, the efficient operation and implementation of several blocks pertaining to cortical implants have been achieved resulting in a fair understanding of block-level engineering issues and their potential solution. Two FDA-approved implantable systems for epilepsy control exists at the moment of writing. Both have received their approval recently, and thus offer solutions that may be improved in very significant ways. A true system-level approach is needed to enable the systematic study and further development of cortical implants. 
The current project is proposed as the one-year continuation of the currently SNSF-funded project No. 200021-157090 named “Implantable Autonomous Wireless Bio-Electronics for High-Resolution Monitoring and Detection of Epilepsy in-vivo,” and formally existing over 03/2015 through 03/2018, and aims at extending and confirming the results obtained towards a system-level implementation. The founding hypothesis of the proposed research remains consisting of the necessity to provide the implant time-based partial autonomy with respect to its external base station. For example, the patients must be allowed to disconnect themselves from the external base station for several hours while trusting the implant to continue operation in automatically adapted state. This fundamental constraint on the operation of the dual consisting of the external base station and the implanted system has tremendous impact on the way the system must be conceived and controlled. The additional one-year research intends to construct over the results that have been achieved during the first three year of research in order to complete the research along the following lines of research and aiming at the additional following scientific goals
•Goal 1, study of power optimization modes of the internal and external sub-modules, and implementation of the algorithms into MCU and FPGA;
•Goal 2, general control of the internal and external sub-modules;
•Goal 3, epilepsy feature extraction classification based on support vector machine, and its implementation into FPGA as part of the internal sub-module;
•Goal 4, study of analog front-end techniques enabling simultaneous stimulation and recording;
•Goal 5, study, design and test of the external unit by using discrete components;
•Goal 6, test in-vitro and then in-vivo of the narrow band downlink data communication from the external unit to the implanted central system;
•Goal 7, test in-vitro and then in-vivo of the narrow band and of the ultra-wide band uplink data communication from the implanted central system to the external unit;
•Goal 8, design and test of the battery charger by using commercial components. Selection of a medical grade rechargeable battery;
•Goal 9, animal experiments studying the effectiveness of high-density readout electrodes, the developed patches, and study of epileptogenic signal capture and seizure detection using the proposed system.
The project aims at gathering the theoretical and development parts into one comprehensive system and methodology, that is applied to epilepsy study in-vivo and that will conduct to implantable system with the capacity of modulating or suppressing seizures. This research project is presented as part of long-term goals of the research groups, which have been active and have collaborated in the domain for several years.

Cardiac arrhythmias have a prevalence of 3.2-6.6% in elderly population (65 to 73 years). Atrial fibrillation (AF), the most common arrhythmia, affects more than 10 million individuals in Europe, and is expected to increase, up to 17.9 million people by 2060. The associated healthcare costs worldwide add up to several 100 billion USD. People suffering from cardiac arrhythmias are prone to syncope, fatigue or even sudden death from ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation. Because of the asymptomatic and intermittent nature of several arrhythmias in their early stages, they are often diagnosed too late, when a patient is hospitalized for complications such as heart failure or stroke. Long-term, continuous ambulatory monitoring of cardiac function is therefore crucial for early arrhythmias detection and classification in order to provide proper diagnosis, treatment and reduce risks of complications. However, cardiac monitoring with current ambulatory electrocardiogram (ECG) devices are cumbersome and limited to a couple of days. Recent advances in photoplethysmography (PPG) have made this technology a promising tool to detect irregular heart rate in ambulatory settings. Because of its non-invasiveness and its unobtrusiveness (e.g. measurement via a simple smartwatch), PPG is promising for long-term ambulatory screening of arrhythmias and more specifically for intermittent AF and non-sustained arrhythmias. 
The main goal of the present project is to develop a robust and reliable algorithm to detect and classify arrhythmias in everyday life with a PPG-based sensor. Important issues need to be addressed to efficiently diagnose arrhythmias in ambulatory settings. First, correct discrimination of different types of arrhythmias require in-depth investigations since certain can easily be confounded (e.g. intermittent AF vs frequent ectopic beats). This explains why current commercial products do not claim to diagnose AF but rather detect abnormal rhythm. For this purpose, specificities of PPG waveforms during arrhythmia occurrences will be analyzed and relevant features will be selected to optimize the classification of each type of arrhythmia. Second, PPG signals are known to be sensitive to motion artifacts or device-skin pressure, which may confound PPG-based arrhythmia detection. To address this issue, the present project will investigate methods to increase the robustness of arrhythmia detection against artifacts, which occur naturally in ambulatory recordings. 
This research will investigate the PPG signal morphology related to physiological and non-physiological changes as well as the relevance of PPG-based features to discriminate arrhythmias. To this end, clinical data will be recorded on patients by using a PPG wrist monitor simultaneously with a 12-lead ECG and intracardiac signals at the university hospital in Lausanne (CHUV). Then, ambulatory data will be recorded on patients using the same PPG and a Holter device at the university hospital in Bern (Inselspital). Our project is divided into four work packages (WP1 to WP4) with the following objectives: 
WP1) To collect comprehensive and high-quality datasets including arrhythmia episodes of at least 10 different types. To do so, we perform three clinical studies on arrhythmia patients: one study during catheter ablation procedures (controlled conditions), and two long-term studies in ambulatory settings (experimental conditions); 
WP2) To gain a deeper understanding of the PPG signal and improve the design of dedicated PPG wrist devices;
WP3) To develop a robust signal quality index to reliably detect and reject artifacts occurring in ambulatory settings;
WP4) To investigate advanced PPG signal features to better discriminate between the different types of arrhythmias, using advanced machine learning techniques. 
The outcome of the present use-inspired project is a novel framework to reliably detect and classify arrhythmias in ambulatory settings based on PPG. This development paves the way to an effective low-cost diagnostic tool for early screening of arrhythmias. Considering the predicted increase of arrhythmia prevalence, our solution is of crucial benefit for today’s ageing populations and aims to reduce the steadily rising of healthcare costs. Furthermore, the high availability of PPG (e.g. via a smartphone camera) makes our solution accessible to the broader public, including low-income countries.

Background: In several complex systems, such as the transportation network, manufacturing or drug dose management, deriving an accurate physics-based model of the system is challenging due to the scale of the system and the uncertainties in the parameters and processes involved. Meanwhile, with the explosion of sensing and data processing capabilities, we can collect data from the systems at an unprecedented rate. This has resulted in a paradigm shift in control: from physics-based to data-driven. However, unless we prove safety of the data-driven approaches, we cannot employ them in safety-critical tasks such as autonomous driving, robot-human interactions or personalized medicine. 

Safe data-driven control problem: With the advent of data-driven control, the problem of safe data-driven control has gained an increasing attention. This problem can be formulated as control of a dynamical system under state and input constraints, with the caveat that the dynamical model or the constraints are a priori uncertain.

Open challenges: Past work on safe data-driven control has mainly focused on the case of unknown dynamics but known constraints. The case in which the constraint sets themselves are being learned based on data is not sufficiently explored. This realistic case arises in several applications including autonomous driving, where the trajectories of nearby traffic are uncertain; autonomous robots operating in unexplored environments or along humans/other robots; or personalized medicine, where the effects of a drug on a given patient is a priori unknown. 

Goal and specific aims: The goal of this project is to develop the theory and algorithms for safe data-driven control, under uncertainties in the safe sets and their evolution. The specific aims are as follows:

1. Developing fundamental understanding of safe data-driven control by characterizing its sample complexity;

2. Modeling probability distributions capturing the uncertainties in an estimation module used to learn safe sets;

3. Synthesizing controllers that ensure satisfaction of  suitably defined notions of risk and safety;

4. Verification of the algorithms on realistic simulation test-beds and on an in-house experimental platform.  

Expected outcome: I will contribute to both the scientific advancement of the problem and the training  of researchers. I will publish the scientific results in strictly peer-reviewed conference and journal venues, across automatic control and machine learning communities, and publicize them through international talks. I will work closely with my trainees to  build their fundamental optimization, control and learning technical skills and to support them in scientific communication. Additionally, I will organize workshops and online courses pertinent to the research objectives and outcomes, to exchange ideas and disseminate the research to a larger scientific community.  

Impact: The future  automated systems, e.g. in intelligent transportation and personalized medicine, need to ensure safety and efficiency despite uncertainties arising from coupled subsystems, human interactions and partial data. Given the importance of this problem, the control and learning communities have been increasingly focusing on it,  making breakthroughs on various formulations of this problem. My proposed research complements these efforts by bridging tools from stochastic control, online optimization and learning, all within my past contributions and expertise, to guarantee safety in a practically important but theoretically less-explored problem class, namely,  uncertain safe sets. It advances fundamental understanding for this problem class, designs robust  control algorithms leveraging this understanding, and offers a holistic approach by verifying and benchmarking the theoretical results on realistic test-beds.
